TIME(2)                        P32W Unix                         TIME(2)

NAME
       time, stime - get or set the time

SYNOPSIS
       #include <time.h>

       time_t time(time_t *t);
       int stime(time_t *t);

DESCRIPTION
       time returns the time as the number of seconds since
       1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC. If t is not a null pointer, the
       time is stored in *t as well.

       stime sets the system's time to *t (note: a pointer, as in
       V7). The battery-backed real-time clock is set too, so the
       time survives a reboot. Only the super-user may call it.

       The kernel keeps UTC; ctime and localtime (time(3)) turn it
       into local time following the TZ environment variable or
       /etc/TZ (tz(5)).

RETURN VALUE
       time returns the time; stime returns 0. On an error both
       return -1 with errno set.

ERRORS
       EFAULT  t is a bad address.
       EPERM   (stime) the caller is not the super-user.

SEE ALSO
       date(1), times(2), time(3), tz(5)

BUGS
       The time is a signed 32-bit number of seconds and goes
       wrong in 2038. There is no gettimeofday: the clock has
       one-second resolution (times(2) counts 1/50 s ticks).
